|~ `::string` and what is declared under it: its constructor and the
   refusal of a payload of another kind, and the words that run on a
   string. ~|

:string
  |~~ A string is text, kept as it came: the value of a JSON string, a
      line of a log, a message. Text is never read as another kind by
      its look, so `"42"` stays text until `parseJson` reads it, and
      `"draft"` is no keyword until `keyword` makes one.

      It is written in double quotes with the escapes of JSON, `\"`,
      `\\`, `\n`, `\t` and `\uXXXX`, any other character raw between
      them, `"line one\nline two"`, and JSON writes it the same.
      `::string"s"` is `"s"`. Strings rank after numbers and before
      keywords, by their characters.

      Its words read it whole or by its characters, `count`, `take`,
      `drop`, `contains`, `startsWith`, `upper`, and break it, `split`
      and `lines`, into a vector that `join` makes text again. `print`
      turns any value into the text of its literal, `5 | print`
      answering `"5"`, and `json` into JSON.
      ::snippet~(::string | manifest) lists its words and refusals.
      ::link~(::keyword | doc) ::link~(::any/print | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|

:string/isAString
  |~~ Text in quotes is a string. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | type | eq ::string)

:string/isMadeByPrint
  |~~ A value of another kind becomes text by `print`. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | print | eq "5")

:string/buildsAString
  |~~ The constructor of a string answers it. ~~|
  ::law~(::string"x" | eq "x")

:string/readsTheEscapesOfJson
  |~~ A tab and a quote are written by their escapes. ~~|
  ::law~("A\t\"" | eq "A	\"")

:string/readsAUnicodeEscape
  |~~ A unicode escape writes its letter. ~~|
  ::law~("\u0041" | eq "A")

:string/construct
  |~~ Reads a string as itself. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/PayloadNotStringError
  |~~ `::string` reads its payload as the string it is; a value of
      another kind becomes text by `print`.
  ~~|

:string/PayloadNotStringError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number payload is refused; `print` makes text of a value. ~~|
  ::law~(::string(1) !| [type /actualType] | eq [::string/PayloadNotStringError ::number])

:string/gt
  |~~ Answers whether the subject comes after the string the call gives,
      the two compared code unit by code unit, `"b" | gt "a"`. So an
      upper-case letter comes before every lower-case one, `"B" | gt
      "a"` answering `false`; a letter outside the ASCII letters comes
      after them, `"é" | gt "z"`; a string comes after its own beginning,
      `"abc" | gt "ab"`; and digits compare as characters, not as
      numbers, `"10" | gt "9"` answering `false`. A string compares with
      a string: a number or a keyword given is refused. A keyword or a
      number in the pipe compares by the `gt` of its own kind, and a
      vector, a set, a map or null answers no `gt`, which the refusal of
      the call names with the kinds that do. A string under a tag
      compares as the string beneath. `gte` answers whether the subject
      comes after or equals, `lt` and `lte` the reverse.
      ::link~(::string/gte | doc) ::link~(::string/lt | doc)
      ::link~(::string/lte | doc) ::link~(::number/gt | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/gt/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject comes after the value, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:string/gt/value
  |~~ The string the subject is compared with, computed against the
      subject, `"b" | gt "a"`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/gt/value/NotStringError
  |~~ The value compared is a string; a keyword gives its name by
      `keyword` first, `"b" | gt (:a | keyword)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string)

:string/gt/ordersByCharacters
  |~~ The string b comes after the string a. ~~|
  ::law~("b" | gt "a")

:string/gt/putsTheUpperCaseFirst
  |~~ An upper-case letter comes before every lower-case one. ~~|
  ::law~("B" | gt "a" | not)

:string/gt/putsALetterBeyondAsciiAfter
  |~~ A letter outside the ASCII letters comes after them. ~~|
  ::law~("é" | gt "z")

:string/gt/followsItsOwnBeginning
  |~~ A string comes after its own beginning. ~~|
  ::law~("abc" | gt "ab")

:string/gt/comparesDigitsAsCharacters
  |~~ Digits compare as characters, not as numbers. ~~|
  ::law~("10" | gt "9" | not)

:string/gt/comparesTheStringUnderATag
  |~~ A string under a tag compares as the string beneath. ~~|
  ::law~(::Name("b") | gt "a")

:string/gt/value/NotStringError/refusesAKeyword
  |~~ A keyword is no string to compare with. ~~|
  ::law~("a" | gt :a !| type | eq ::string/gt/value/NotStringError)

:string/gt/value/NotStringError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no string to compare with. ~~|
  ::law~("a" | gt 1 !| type | eq ::string/gt/value/NotStringError)

:string/gt/namesTheKindsThatAnswerIt
  |~~ Null answers no `gt`, and the refusal names the kinds that do. ~~|
  ::law~(null | gt "a" !| /addresses | eq #[::desc/gt ::keyword/gt ::number/gt ::string/gt ::tag/gt])

:string/gte
  |~~ Answers whether the subject comes after the string the call gives
      or equals it, in the order `gt` tells, `"b" | gte "b"`.
      ::link~(::string/gt | doc) ::link~(::string/lte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/gte/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject comes after the value or equals it, `false`
      otherwise.
  ~~|
  ::boolean

:string/gte/value
  |~~ The string the subject is compared with, computed against the
      subject, `"b" | gte "a"`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/gte/value/NotStringError
  |~~ The value compared is a string; a keyword gives its name by
      `keyword` first, `"b" | gte (:a | keyword)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string)

:string/gte/holdsOfItself
  |~~ A string comes after or equals itself. ~~|
  ::law~("b" | gte "b")

:string/gte/failsOfALaterString
  |~~ A string does not come after a later one. ~~|
  ::law~("a" | gte "b" | not)

:string/gte/value/NotStringError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no string to compare with. ~~|
  ::law~("a" | gte 1 !| type | eq ::string/gte/value/NotStringError)

:string/lt
  |~~ Answers whether the subject comes before the string the call gives,
      in the order `gt` tells, `"a" | lt "b"`; the empty string comes
      before every other.
      ::link~(::string/gt | doc) ::link~(::string/lte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/lt/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject comes before the value, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:string/lt/value
  |~~ The string the subject is compared with, computed against the
      subject, `"b" | lt "a"`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/lt/value/NotStringError
  |~~ The value compared is a string; a keyword gives its name by
      `keyword` first, `"b" | lt (:a | keyword)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string)

:string/lt/ordersByCharacters
  |~~ The string a comes before the string b. ~~|
  ::law~("a" | lt "b")

:string/lt/putsTheEmptyStringFirst
  |~~ The empty string comes before every other. ~~|
  ::law~("" | lt "a")

:string/lt/value/NotStringError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no string to compare with. ~~|
  ::law~("a" | lt 1 !| type | eq ::string/lt/value/NotStringError)

:string/lt/failsAfter
  |~~ A string after the value is not before it. ~~|
  ::law~("b" | lt "a" | not)

:string/lte
  |~~ Answers whether the subject comes before the string the call gives
      or equals it, in the order `gt` tells, `"a" | lte "a"`.
      ::link~(::string/lt | doc) ::link~(::string/gte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/lte/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject comes before the value or equals it, `false`
      otherwise.
  ~~|
  ::boolean

:string/lte/value
  |~~ The string the subject is compared with, computed against the
      subject, `"b" | lte "a"`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/lte/value/NotStringError
  |~~ The value compared is a string; a keyword gives its name by
      `keyword` first, `"b" | lte (:a | keyword)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string)

:string/lte/holdsOfItself
  |~~ A string comes before or equals itself. ~~|
  ::law~("a" | lte "a")

:string/lte/failsOfAnEarlierString
  |~~ A string does not come before an earlier one. ~~|
  ::law~("b" | lte "a" | not)

:string/lte/value/NotStringError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no string to compare with. ~~|
  ::law~("a" | lte 1 !| type | eq ::string/lte/value/NotStringError)

:string/count
  |~~ Answers how many characters the string holds, a letter of any
      script one, `"привет" | count` answering `6`, and the empty string
      none. A character is a code point: an emoji of one code point counts
      one, while a skin tone, a flag or a joined emoji counts each of its
      code points, and a letter written with a combining accent counts
      two, the letter and the accent. The name `count` also counts the elements of a vector
      or a set and the entries of a map, and a number or null answers no
      `count`, which the refusal of the call names with the kinds that
      do.
      ::link~(::string/take | doc) ::link~(::vec/count | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/count/answer
  |~~ How many characters the string holds. ~~|
  ::number

:string/count/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `count` counts every character of the string and takes no value:
      the occurrences of a piece count by `split`, `"banana" | split "a"
      | count | sub 1` answering `3`.
  ~~|

:string/count/countsCharacters
  |~~ Three letters count three. ~~|
  ::law~("abc" | count | eq 3)

:string/count/countsALetterOfAnyScriptOne
  |~~ A letter of any script counts one. ~~|
  ::law~("привет" | count | eq 6)

:string/count/countsNoneInTheEmptyString
  |~~ The empty string counts none. ~~|
  ::law~("" | count | eq 0)

:string/count/countsAnEmojiOne
  |~~ An emoji is one code point and counts one. ~~|
  ::law~("😀" | count | eq 1)

:string/count/countsEachCodePointOfATonedEmoji
  |~~ An emoji with a skin tone counts its two code points. ~~|
  ::law~("\ud83d\udc4d\ud83c\udffd" | count | eq 2)

:string/count/countsACombiningAccentApart
  |~~ A letter with a combining accent counts two. ~~|
  ::law~("e\u0301" | count | eq 2)

:string/count/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesAPiece
  |~~ A piece given to `count` is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~("banana" | count "a" !| type | eq ::string/count/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:string/count/namesTheKindsThatAnswerIt
  |~~ A number answers no `count`, and the refusal names the kinds that do. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | count !| /addresses | eq #[::map/count ::set/count ::string/count ::vec/count])

:string/take
  |~~ Answers the first characters of the string, as many as the count
      the call gives, `"db_host" | take 2` answering `"db"`. A count past
      the end takes the whole string, and a count of zero or below takes
      none, the empty string. A character is a code point, as `count`
      counts it, so an emoji of one code point is taken whole. The count is a whole number:
      a fraction and a string are refused. A string under a tag answers
      under the tag. The name `take` also takes the first elements of a
      vector, a set or a map.
      ::link~(::string/drop | doc) ::link~(::string/count | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/take | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/take/answer
  |~~ The first characters of the string, under the tags of the subject. ~~|
  ::string

:string/take/count
  |~~ How many characters to take, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/take/count/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count is a number, `"abc" | take 2`; text that holds one is
      read by `parseJson` first, `take ("2" | parseJson)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:string/take/count/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The count is a whole number, `"abc" | take 2`; a fraction is
      rounded first, `take (1.5 | round)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:string/take/takesTheFirstCharacters
  |~~ Two characters of a string are its first two. ~~|
  ::law~("db_host" | take 2 | eq "db")

:string/take/takesNoMoreThanThereAre
  |~~ A count past the end takes the whole string. ~~|
  ::law~("ab" | take 5 | eq "ab")

:string/take/takesNoneBelowZero
  |~~ A count below zero takes none. ~~|
  ::law~("abc" | take -1 | eq "")

:string/take/takesAnEmojiWhole
  |~~ An emoji is one character, taken whole. ~~|
  ::law~("😀x" | take 1 | eq "😀")

:string/take/answersUnderTheTagOfTheSubject
  |~~ A string under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Name("ab") | take 1 | eq ::Name("a"))

:string/take/count/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A count written as a string is refused. ~~|
  ::law~("abc" | take "2" !| type | eq ::string/take/count/NotNumberError)

:string/take/count/NotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
  |~~ A fraction of a count is refused. ~~|
  ::law~("abc" | take 1.5 !| type | eq ::string/take/count/NotIntegerError)

:string/drop
  |~~ Answers the string without its first characters, as many as the
      count the call gives, the rest after a prefix of known length,
      `"db_host" | drop 3` answering `"host"`. A count past the end leaves
      the empty string, and a count of zero or below leaves the whole
      string. A character is a code point, as `count` counts it, so an
      emoji is dropped whole. The count is a whole number. A string under
      a tag answers under the tag. The name `drop` also drops the first
      elements of a vector, a set or a map.
      ::link~(::string/take | doc) ::link~(::vec/drop | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/drop/answer
  |~~ The characters after the ones dropped, under the tags of the
      subject.
  ~~|
  ::string

:string/drop/count
  |~~ How many characters to drop, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/drop/count/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count is a number, `"abc" | drop 2`; text that holds one is
      read by `parseJson` first, `drop ("2" | parseJson)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:string/drop/count/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The count is a whole number, `"abc" | drop 2`; a fraction is
      rounded first, `drop (1.5 | round)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:string/drop/dropsAPrefix
  |~~ Dropping a prefix of known length leaves the rest. ~~|
  ::law~("db_host" | drop 3 | eq "host")

:string/drop/leavesNothingPastTheEnd
  |~~ A count past the end leaves the empty string. ~~|
  ::law~("abc" | drop 99 | eq "")

:string/drop/leavesTheWholeStringBelowZero
  |~~ A count below zero leaves the whole string. ~~|
  ::law~("abc" | drop -1 | eq "abc")

:string/drop/dropsAnEmojiWhole
  |~~ An emoji is one character, dropped whole. ~~|
  ::law~("😀x" | drop 1 | eq "x")

:string/drop/answersUnderTheTagOfTheSubject
  |~~ A string under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Name("ab") | drop 1 | eq ::Name("b"))

:string/drop/count/NotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
  |~~ A fraction of a count is refused. ~~|
  ::law~("abc" | drop 0.5 !| type | eq ::string/drop/count/NotIntegerError)

:string/drop/count/NotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A count written as a string is refused. ~~|
  ::law~("abc" | drop "1" !| type | eq ::string/drop/count/NotNumberError)

:string/split
  |~~ Answers the pieces of the string between the occurrences of the
      separator the call gives, in order, a vector of strings: `"a,b,c" |
      split ","` answers `["a" "b" "c"]`. Two separators side by side
      leave an empty piece between them, a separator at an end an empty
      piece there, and the empty string is one empty piece; a separator
      the string lacks leaves it whole, one piece. The separator is any
      string, a line break or `"::"` among them, and a keyword is
      refused. The empty separator cuts the string into its characters,
      as `count` counts them: a letter stands alone, and an emoji
      of one code point stays whole. `join` writes the pieces back, and
      `lines` breaks a text at its line breaks. A vector of strings
      splits by `map`, each element into a vector of its own.
      ::link~(::string/lines | doc) ::link~(::vec/join | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/split/answer
  |~~ The pieces between the separators, in order, each a string. ~~|
  ::vec

:string/split/separator
  |~~ The string the subject is cut at, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/split/separator/NotStringError
  |~~ The separator is a string; a keyword gives its name by `keyword`. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string)

:string/split/separator/NotGivenError
  |~~ `split` needs the separator to cut at, `split ","`: a text cuts at
      its spaces by `split " "`, and at its line breaks by `lines`.
  ~~|

:string/split/SubjectNotStringError
  |~~ `split` cuts a string: a vector of strings splits by `map`,
      `map ~(split ",")`, and a value becomes text by `print`.
  ~~|

:string/split/cutsAtTheSeparator
  |~~ The pieces between the commas. ~~|
  ::law~("a,b,c" | split "," | eq ["a" "b" "c"])

:string/split/leavesAnEmptyPieceBetweenTwoSeparators
  |~~ Two separators side by side leave an empty piece between them. ~~|
  ::law~("a,,b" | split "," | eq ["a" "" "b"])

:string/split/leavesAnEmptyPieceAtAnEnd
  |~~ A separator at an end leaves an empty piece there. ~~|
  ::law~("a,b," | split "," | eq ["a" "b" ""])

:string/split/cutsTheEmptyStringIntoOneEmptyPiece
  |~~ The empty string is one empty piece. ~~|
  ::law~("" | split "," | eq [""])

:string/split/leavesAStringWithoutTheSeparatorWhole
  |~~ A separator the string lacks leaves it whole. ~~|
  ::law~("a,b" | split ";" | eq ["a,b"])

:string/split/cutsAtALineBreak
  |~~ A line break separates as any separator does. ~~|
  ::law~("line1\nline2" | split "\n" | eq ["line1" "line2"])

:string/split/cutsAtASeparatorOfSeveralCharacters
  |~~ A separator may be longer than one character. ~~|
  ::law~("a::b" | split "::" | eq ["a" "b"])

:string/split/cutsEveryLetterApartAtTheEmptySeparator
  |~~ The empty separator leaves every letter alone. ~~|
  ::law~("abc" | split "" | eq ["a" "b" "c"])

:string/split/keepsAnEmojiWholeAtTheEmptySeparator
  |~~ The empty separator keeps an emoji of one code point whole. ~~|
  ::law~("😀x" | split "" | eq ["😀" "x"])

:string/split/readsBackByJoin
  |~~ `join` writes the pieces back. ~~|
  ::law~("a,b" | split "," | join "," | eq "a,b")

:string/split/splitsEachElementByMap
  |~~ A vector of strings splits by `map`, each into a vector of its own. ~~|
  ::law~(["a,b" "c"] | map ~(split ",") | eq [["a" "b"] ["c"]])

:string/split/SubjectNotStringError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no string to cut. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | split "," !| [type /actualType] | eq [::string/split/SubjectNotStringError ::number])

:string/split/separator/NotStringError/refusesAKeyword
  |~~ A keyword is no separator. ~~|
  ::law~("abc" | split :b !| type | eq ::string/split/separator/NotStringError)

:string/split/separator/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoSeparator
  |~~ A call with no separator is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~("abc" | split !| type | eq ::string/split/separator/NotGivenError)

:string/lines
  |~~ Answers the lines of the text, a vector of strings without their
      breaks: `"north\nsouth" | lines` answers `["north" "south"]`. A
      `\n` ends a line and a `\r` before it belongs to the ending, while
      a `\r` alone ends no line; a break at the end closes the last line
      rather than opening an empty one, so a text reads alike with and
      without it; an empty line between two breaks stays; and the empty
      text holds no line. The text is then worked on as a vector, and
      `join "\n"` writes it back without its closing break.

      `lines` breaks a string, the text the command line reads with
      `--raw`, and takes no value; a vector of texts breaks element by
      element by `map`, `map ~lines`.
      ::link~(::string/split | doc) ::link~(::vec/join | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/lines/answer
  |~~ The lines of the text in order, without their breaks. ~~|
  ::vec

:string/lines/SubjectNotStringError
  |~~ `lines` breaks a string: a vector of texts breaks by `map`,
      `map ~lines`, and a value becomes text by `print`.
  ~~|

:string/lines/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `lines` breaks a text at its line breaks alone: a text breaks at
      another separator by `split`, `split ";"`.
  ~~|

:string/lines/splitsAtLineBreaks
  |~~ Each line is a string. ~~|
  ::law~("north\nsouth" | lines | eq ["north" "south"])

:string/lines/takesACarriageReturnWithItsBreak
  |~~ A carriage return before a break belongs to the ending. ~~|
  ::law~("one\r\ntwo" | lines | eq ["one" "two"])

:string/lines/endsNoLineAtACarriageReturnAlone
  |~~ A carriage return alone ends no line. ~~|
  ::law~("a\rb" | lines | count | eq 1)

:string/lines/closesTheLastLineAtAClosingBreak
  |~~ A break at the end opens no empty line. ~~|
  ::law~("one\ntwo\n" | lines | eq ["one" "two"])

:string/lines/keepsAnEmptyLine
  |~~ An empty line between two breaks stays. ~~|
  ::law~("a\n\nb" | lines | eq ["a" "" "b"])

:string/lines/findsNoLineInTheEmptyString
  |~~ The empty string holds no line. ~~|
  ::law~("" | lines | eq [])

:string/lines/writesBackByJoin
  |~~ `join "\n"` writes the lines back. ~~|
  ::law~("a\nb" | lines | join "\n" | eq "a\nb")

:string/lines/breaksEachTextByMap
  |~~ A vector of texts breaks by `map`, each into a vector of its own. ~~|
  ::law~(["a\nb" "c"] | map ~lines | eq [["a" "b"] ["c"]])

:string/lines/SubjectNotStringError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no text. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | lines !| [type /actualType] | eq [::string/lines/SubjectNotStringError ::number])

:string/lines/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesASeparator
  |~~ A separator given to `lines` is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~("a;b" | lines ";" !| type | eq ::string/lines/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:string/lower
  |~~ Answers the string with every letter in lower case, of any script,
      `"Ann@Example.COM" | lower` answering `"ann@example.com"`, so two
      strings that differ in case alone are equal once lowered; digits
      and signs stay as they are. A letter may lower into more than one
      character, `"İ"` into an `i` and a combining dot, so the count may
      grow. A string under a tag answers under the tag. A keyword has no
      case to lower: `keyword` gives its name first. A vector of strings
      lowers by `map`.
      ::link~(::string/upper | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/lower/answer
  |~~ The text in lower case, under the tags of the subject. ~~|
  ::string

:string/lower/SubjectNotStringError
  |~~ `lower` lowers a string: a keyword gives its name by `keyword`,
      `:A | keyword | lower`, and a vector of strings lowers by `map`,
      `map ~lower`.
  ~~|

:string/lower/lowersEveryLetter
  |~~ Every letter becomes lower case. ~~|
  ::law~("Ann@Example.COM" | lower | eq "ann@example.com")

:string/lower/lowersALetterOfAnyScript
  |~~ A letter of any script lowers. ~~|
  ::law~("Ä" | lower | eq "ä")

:string/lower/makesCaseVariantsEqual
  |~~ Two strings that differ in case alone are equal once lowered. ~~|
  ::law~(["Ann" "ANN"] | map ~lower | distinct | count | eq 1)

:string/lower/mayGrowTheCount
  |~~ A letter may lower into more than one character. ~~|
  ::law~("İ" | lower | count | eq 2)

:string/lower/answersUnderTheTagOfTheSubject
  |~~ A string under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Name("Ab") | lower | eq ::Name("ab"))

:string/lower/SubjectNotStringError/refusesAKeyword
  |~~ A keyword has no case to lower. ~~|
  ::law~(:Ab | lower !| type | eq ::string/lower/SubjectNotStringError)

:string/upper
  |~~ Answers the string with every letter in upper case, of any script,
      `"ok, go" | upper` answering `"OK, GO"`; digits and signs stay as
      they are. A letter may raise into more than one character, `ß`
      into `SS`, so the count may grow. A string under a tag answers
      under the tag. A keyword has no case to raise: `keyword` gives its
      name first. A vector of strings raises by `map`.
      ::link~(::string/lower | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/upper/answer
  |~~ The text in upper case, under the tags of the subject. ~~|
  ::string

:string/upper/SubjectNotStringError
  |~~ `upper` raises a string: a keyword gives its name by `keyword`, and
      a vector of strings raises by `map`, `map ~upper`.
  ~~|

:string/upper/raisesEveryLetter
  |~~ Every letter becomes upper case. ~~|
  ::law~("ok, go" | upper | eq "OK, GO")

:string/upper/mayGrowTheCount
  |~~ A letter may raise into more than one character. ~~|
  ::law~("Straße" | upper | eq "STRASSE")

:string/upper/answersUnderTheTagOfTheSubject
  |~~ A string under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Name("ab") | upper | eq ::Name("AB"))

:string/upper/raisesEachElementByMap
  |~~ A vector of strings raises by `map`. ~~|
  ::law~(["a" "b"] | map ~upper | eq ["A" "B"])

:string/upper/SubjectNotStringError/refusesAKeyword
  |~~ A keyword has no case to raise. ~~|
  ::law~(:ab | upper !| type | eq ::string/upper/SubjectNotStringError)

:string/trim
  |~~ Answers the string without the whitespace at its two ends, spaces,
      tabs, line breaks and the no-break space, so a field cut out of a
      line compares as the text it holds; the whitespace inside stays. A
      string under a tag answers under the tag. A vector of strings trims
      by `map`, `split "," | map ~trim`.
      ::link~(::string/split | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/trim/answer
  |~~ The text without the whitespace at its ends, under the tags of the
      subject.
  ~~|
  ::string

:string/trim/SubjectNotStringError
  |~~ `trim` trims a string: a vector of strings trims by `map`,
      `map ~trim`, and a value becomes text by `print`.
  ~~|

:string/trim/dropsTheWhitespaceAtTheEnds
  |~~ The tab, the spaces and the break at the ends go. ~~|
  ::law~("\t db_host \n" | trim | eq "db_host")

:string/trim/dropsTheNoBreakSpace
  |~~ The no-break space at the ends goes too. ~~|
  ::law~("\u00a0a\u00a0" | trim | eq "a")

:string/trim/keepsTheWhitespaceInside
  |~~ The whitespace inside the string stays. ~~|
  ::law~("a  b" | trim | eq "a  b")

:string/trim/cleansTheFieldsOfALine
  |~~ The fields of a line compare as the text they hold once trimmed. ~~|
  ::law~("a, b ,c" | split "," | map ~trim | eq ["a" "b" "c"])

:string/trim/answersUnderTheTagOfTheSubject
  |~~ A string under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Name(" a ") | trim | eq ::Name("a"))

:string/trim/SubjectNotStringError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number has no whitespace to trim. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | trim !| [type /actualType] | eq [::string/trim/SubjectNotStringError ::number])

:string/contains
  |~~ Answers whether the string holds the needle the call gives as a
      piece of its text, anywhere, inside a word among it: `"hello" |
      contains "ell"` answers `true`. Every string holds the empty
      needle. Case counts, `"Hello" | contains "h"` answering `false`;
      where it should not, `lower` comes first, `lower | contains "h"`.
      The needle is a string: a keyword is refused, and `keyword` gives
      its name. The name `contains` asks a vector and a set for an
      element, whole, so `["ab"] | contains "a"` answers `false`, and a
      number or a map answers no `contains`, which the refusal of the
      call names with the kinds that do.
      ::link~(::string/startsWith | doc) ::link~(::string/endsWith | doc)
      ::link~(::vec/contains | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/contains/answer
  |~~ `true` when the text holds the needle, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:string/contains/needle
  |~~ The text looked for, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/contains/needle/NotStringError
  |~~ The needle is a string; a keyword gives its name by `keyword`. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string)

:string/contains/findsAWord
  |~~ A word of the string is found. ~~|
  ::law~("hello world" | contains "world")

:string/contains/findsTextInside
  |~~ Text inside a word is found. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | contains "ell")

:string/contains/missesAbsentText
  |~~ Text the string lacks is not found. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | contains "xyz" | not)

:string/contains/holdsTheEmptyNeedle
  |~~ Every string holds the empty needle. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | contains "")

:string/contains/countsCase
  |~~ Case counts. ~~|
  ::law~("Hello" | contains "h" | not)

:string/contains/ignoresCaseAfterLower
  |~~ Lowered first, the string holds the needle whatever its case. ~~|
  ::law~("Hello" | lower | contains "h")

:string/contains/asksAVectorForAWholeElement
  |~~ A vector of strings is asked for a whole element, not a piece. ~~|
  ::law~(["ab"] | contains "a" | not)

:string/contains/needle/NotStringError/refusesAKeyword
  |~~ A keyword is no needle. ~~|
  ::law~("hi" | contains :h !| type | eq ::string/contains/needle/NotStringError)

:string/contains/namesTheKindsThatAnswerIt
  |~~ A map answers no `contains`, and the refusal names the kinds that do. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | contains "a" !| /addresses | eq #[::set/contains ::string/contains ::vec/contains])

:string/startsWith
  |~~ Answers whether the string begins with the prefix the call gives,
      `"hello world" | startsWith "hello"`. The empty prefix begins every
      string, a prefix longer than the string begins none, and case
      counts. The prefix is a string, and a keyword is refused. Only a
      string answers `startsWith`, and another value is refused: a field
      is read first, `/name | startsWith "a"`, and a vector of strings
      asks by `map`, each element apart.
      ::link~(::string/endsWith | doc) ::link~(::string/contains | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/startsWith/answer
  |~~ `true` when the text begins with the prefix, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:string/startsWith/prefix
  |~~ The text the string begins with, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/startsWith/prefix/NotStringError
  |~~ The prefix is a string, `"abc" | startsWith "ab"`; a keyword gives
      its name by `keyword` first, `startsWith (:ab | keyword)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string)

:string/startsWith/SubjectNotStringError
  |~~ `startsWith` reads the beginning of a string: a field is read
      first, `/name | startsWith "a"`, and a vector of strings asks by
      `map`.
  ~~|

:string/startsWith/findsThePrefix
  |~~ The string begins with its first word. ~~|
  ::law~("hello world" | startsWith "hello")

:string/startsWith/missesAnotherPrefix
  |~~ Another word does not begin it. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | startsWith "world" | not)

:string/startsWith/beginsWithTheEmptyPrefix
  |~~ The empty prefix begins every string. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | startsWith "")

:string/startsWith/missesALongerPrefix
  |~~ A prefix longer than the string begins none. ~~|
  ::law~("hi" | startsWith "hello" | not)

:string/startsWith/countsCase
  |~~ Case counts. ~~|
  ::law~("Hello" | startsWith "h" | not)

:string/startsWith/asksEachElementByMap
  |~~ A vector of strings asks by `map`, each element apart. ~~|
  ::law~(["ab" "ba"] | map ~(startsWith "a") | eq [true false])

:string/startsWith/SubjectNotStringError/refusesAVector
  |~~ A vector is no string to read the beginning of. ~~|
  ::law~(["a"] | startsWith "a" !| type | eq ::string/startsWith/SubjectNotStringError)

:string/startsWith/prefix/NotStringError/refusesAKeyword
  |~~ A keyword is no prefix. ~~|
  ::law~("hi" | startsWith :h !| type | eq ::string/startsWith/prefix/NotStringError)

:string/endsWith
  |~~ Answers whether the string ends with the suffix the call gives,
      `"x.json" | endsWith ".json"`. The empty suffix ends every string,
      a suffix longer than the string ends none, and case counts. The
      suffix is a string, and a keyword is refused. Only a string answers
      `endsWith`, and another value is refused: a field is read first,
      `/file | endsWith ".json"`, and a vector of strings asks by `map`,
      each element apart.
      ::link~(::string/startsWith | doc) ::link~(::string/contains | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/endsWith/answer
  |~~ `true` when the text ends with the suffix, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:string/endsWith/suffix
  |~~ The text the string ends with, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/endsWith/suffix/NotStringError
  |~~ The suffix is a string, `"abc" | endsWith "bc"`; a keyword gives
      its name by `keyword` first, `endsWith (:bc | keyword)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string)

:string/endsWith/SubjectNotStringError
  |~~ `endsWith` reads the end of a string: a field is read first, `/file
      | endsWith ".json"`, and a vector of strings asks by `map`.
  ~~|

:string/endsWith/findsTheSuffix
  |~~ The string ends with its last word. ~~|
  ::law~("hello world" | endsWith "world")

:string/endsWith/missesAnotherSuffix
  |~~ Another word does not end it. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | endsWith "xyz" | not)

:string/endsWith/endsWithTheEmptySuffix
  |~~ The empty suffix ends every string. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | endsWith "")

:string/endsWith/missesALongerSuffix
  |~~ A suffix longer than the string ends none. ~~|
  ::law~("hi" | endsWith "ohi" | not)

:string/endsWith/countsCase
  |~~ Case counts. ~~|
  ::law~("Hello" | endsWith "O" | not)

:string/endsWith/asksEachElementByMap
  |~~ A vector of strings asks by `map`, each element apart. ~~|
  ::law~(["ab" "ba"] | map ~(endsWith "a") | eq [false true])

:string/endsWith/SubjectNotStringError/refusesAVector
  |~~ A vector is no string to read the end of. ~~|
  ::law~(["a"] | endsWith "a" !| type | eq ::string/endsWith/SubjectNotStringError)

:string/endsWith/suffix/NotStringError/refusesAKeyword
  |~~ A keyword is no suffix. ~~|
  ::law~("hi" | endsWith :i !| type | eq ::string/endsWith/suffix/NotStringError)

:string/prepend
  |~~ Answers the string with the prefix the call gives in front of it,
      `"world" | prepend "hello "` answering `"hello world"`. A string
      under a tag answers under the tag. The prefix is a string, or a
      keyword written as its name, `"x" | prepend :a` answering `"ax"`;
      a value of another kind becomes text by `print` first, `prepend (n
      | print)`. The name `prepend` also puts an element in front of a
      vector, `[1] | prepend 0`.
      ::link~(::string/append | doc) ::link~(::vec/prepend | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/prepend/answer
  |~~ The text with the prefix before it, under the tags of the subject. ~~|
  ::string

:string/prepend/prefix
  |~~ The text put in front, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/prepend/prefix/NotStringOrKeywordError
  |~~ The prefix is a string, or a keyword written as its name; `print`
      makes text of another value.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string ::keyword)

:string/prepend/putsThePrefixInFront
  |~~ The prefix goes in front. ~~|
  ::law~("world" | prepend "hello " | eq "hello world")

:string/prepend/keepsTheTag
  |~~ A string under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box("b") | prepend "a" | eq ::Box("ab"))

:string/prepend/prependsTheTextOfAValue
  |~~ A value becomes text by `print` before it goes in front. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | prepend (42 | print) | eq "42x")

:string/prepend/writesAKeywordAsItsName
  |~~ A keyword goes in front as its name. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | prepend :a | eq "ax")

:string/prepend/prefix/NotStringOrKeywordError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number prefix is refused; `print` makes it text. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | prepend 42 !| type | eq ::string/prepend/prefix/NotStringOrKeywordError)

:string/append
  |~~ Answers the string with the suffix the call gives after it, `"hello"
      | append " world"` answering `"hello world"`. A string under a tag
      answers under the tag. The suffix is a string, or a keyword written
      as its name, `"x" | append :a` answering `"xa"`; a value of another
      kind becomes text by `print` first, `append (n | print)`. The name
      `append` also puts an element after the last of a vector, `[1] |
      append 2`.
      ::link~(::string/prepend | doc) ::link~(::vec/append | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/append/answer
  |~~ The text with the suffix after it, under the tags of the subject. ~~|
  ::string

:string/append/suffix
  |~~ The text put after, computed against the subject. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:string/append/suffix/NotStringOrKeywordError
  |~~ The suffix is a string, or a keyword written as its name; `print`
      makes text of another value.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::string ::keyword)

:string/append/putsTheSuffixAfter
  |~~ The suffix goes after. ~~|
  ::law~("hello" | append " world" | eq "hello world")

:string/append/keepsTheTag
  |~~ A string under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box("a") | append "b" | eq ::Box("ab"))

:string/append/appendsTheTextOfAValue
  |~~ A value becomes text by `print` before it goes after. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | append (42 | print) | eq "x42")

:string/append/writesAKeywordAsItsName
  |~~ A keyword goes after as its name. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | append :a | eq "xa")

:string/append/suffix/NotStringOrKeywordError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number suffix is refused; `print` makes it text. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | append 42 !| type | eq ::string/append/suffix/NotStringOrKeywordError)

:string/parseJson
  |~~ Answers the value the JSON text of the string spells, the reverse
      of `json`: an object is a map whose keys are keywords, a key with
      a space among them quoted, an array a vector, and a number, a
      string, `true`, `false` and `null` themselves; whitespace around
      the text is read past, and a key written twice keeps its last
      value. A string that holds a number reads as the number, `"42" |
      parseJson`, which is how a number comes out of text. JSON has no
      form for a set, a keyword or a tag: what `json` wrote of a set
      reads back as a vector, of a keyword as a string, and of a value
      under a tag as a map of its `$tag` and `payload`, and of an error
      as a map of its `$error`. Text that is no JSON is refused with the message of the
      reader of JSON, and a number past the doubles is refused with the
      path to it in the text.
      ::link~(::any/json | doc) ::link~(::string/parse | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/parseJson/answer
  |~~ The value the JSON text spells, of whatever kind it is. ~~|
  ::any

:string/parseJson/SubjectNotStringError
  |~~ `parseJson` reads a string of JSON text; a value becomes such text
      by `json`.
  ~~|

:string/parseJson/TextNotJsonError
  |~~ `parseJson` could not read the subject as JSON. The message of the
      reader of JSON rides on `:message`.
  ~~|

:string/parseJson/TextNotJsonError/message
  |~~ What the reader of JSON says of the text. ~~|
  ::string

:string/parseJson/NumberNotFiniteError
  |~~ JSON text may write a number past the range of the doubles a
      number of qlang is, `1e400`, which no number of the language holds,
      so reading it is refused, no number of the language holding it. It
      is met where JSON text is read, by `parseJson` and by the command
      line reading its input; a literal of qlang past the range is the
      parser's own refusal.
  ~~|

:string/parseJson/NumberNotFiniteError/path
  |~~ The path to the number in the text. ~~|
  ::vec

:string/parseJson/readsANumber
  |~~ A JSON number reads as a number. ~~|
  ::law~("42" | parseJson | eq 42)

:string/parseJson/readsAString
  |~~ A JSON string reads as a string. ~~|
  ::law~("\"hello\"" | parseJson | eq "hello")

:string/parseJson/readsAnArrayAsAVector
  |~~ A JSON array reads as a vector. ~~|
  ::law~("[1,2,3]" | parseJson | eq [1 2 3])

:string/parseJson/readsAnObjectAsAMap
  |~~ A JSON object reads as a map of keywords. ~~|
  ::law~("{\"name\":\"alice\"}" | parseJson | eq {:name "alice"})

:string/parseJson/quotesAKeyWithASpace
  |~~ A key with a space reads as a quoted keyword. ~~|
  ::law~("{\"first name\": 1}" | parseJson | eq {:"first name" 1})

:string/parseJson/readsTheLiteralsAsThemselves
  |~~ JSON true, false and null read as themselves. ~~|
  ::law~("[true, false, null]" | parseJson | eq [true false null])

:string/parseJson/readsPastTheWhitespaceAround
  |~~ Whitespace around the text is read past. ~~|
  ::law~(" 42 " | parseJson | eq 42)

:string/parseJson/keepsTheLastOfAKeyWrittenTwice
  |~~ A key written twice keeps its last value. ~~|
  ::law~("{\"a\":1,\"a\":2}" | parseJson | eq {:a 2})

:string/parseJson/readsBackWhatJsonWrote
  |~~ What `json` wrote of a map of vectors reads back as it was. ~~|
  ::law~({:a [1 "x"]} | json | parseJson | eq {:a [1 "x"]})

:string/parseJson/readsASetBackAsAVector
  |~~ What `json` wrote of a set reads back as a vector. ~~|
  ::law~(#[1 2] | json | parseJson | eq [1 2])

:string/parseJson/readsAKeywordBackAsAString
  |~~ What `json` wrote of a keyword reads back as a string. ~~|
  ::law~(:a | json | parseJson | eq "a")

:string/parseJson/readsATagBackAsAMap
  |~~ What `json` wrote of a value under a tag reads back as a map. ~~|
  ::law~(::Box("a") | json | parseJson | eq {:"$tag" "Box" :payload "a"})

:string/parseJson/SubjectNotStringError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no text to read. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | parseJson
    !| [type /actualType]
    |  eq [::string/parseJson/SubjectNotStringError ::number])

:string/parseJson/TextNotJsonError/refusesBrokenText
  |~~ Text that is no JSON is refused. ~~|
  ::law~("{not json" | parseJson !| type | eq ::string/parseJson/TextNotJsonError)

:string/parseJson/NumberNotFiniteError/refusesANumberPastTheRange
  |~~ A number past the range of doubles is refused. ~~|
  ::law~("1e400" | parseJson !| type | eq ::string/parseJson/NumberNotFiniteError)

:string/parseJson/NumberNotFiniteError/namesThePathToTheNumber
  |~~ The refusal names the path to the number in the text. ~~|
  ::law~("[1, 1e400]" | parseJson !| /path | eq [1])

:string/keyword
  |~~ Answers the keyword the string names, `"draft" | keyword` answering
      `:draft`: the bridge from strings, JSON data and text built from
      parts, to keywords, the keys of maps and the names of a scope, so
      `at ("a" | keyword)` reads the key a string names. Any text names a
      keyword: a slash makes a path, `:qlang/error`, and a space or the
      empty string makes a quoted one, `:"a b"`. A keyword answers its
      name by `keyword` again, so the two read back. A string under a tag
      names the keyword of its text.
      ::link~(::keyword/keyword | doc) ::link~(::keyword | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/keyword/answer
  |~~ The keyword the text names. ~~|
  ::keyword

:string/keyword/namesAKeyword
  |~~ A string names the keyword of its text. ~~|
  ::law~("foo" | keyword | eq :foo)

:string/keyword/keepsASlash
  |~~ A slash stays in the name. ~~|
  ::law~("qlang/error" | keyword | eq :qlang/error)

:string/keyword/quotesAName
  |~~ A space makes a quoted keyword. ~~|
  ::law~("a b" | keyword | eq :"a b")

:string/keyword/readsTheKeyAStringNames
  |~~ A string names the key a map is read by. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | at ("a" | keyword) | eq 1)

:string/keyword/readsBack
  |~~ A keyword's name reads back as the string. ~~|
  ::law~("bar" | keyword | keyword | eq "bar")

:string/keyword/namesTheKeywordOfATaggedString
  |~~ A string under a tag names the keyword of its text. ~~|
  ::law~(::Name("ab") | keyword | eq :ab)

:string/parse
  |~~ Answers the quote of the steps the source text spells, `"add 1 2" |
      parse` answering `~(add 1 2)`, the reverse of `parse` on a quote,
      which answers its text; the empty text spells the empty quote.
      `apply` runs the quote on a subject, `5 | apply ("add 1" | parse)`,
      and a source that starts from a value of its own runs by `apply /`,
      so source text is evaluated without leaving the language. A source
      the parser cannot read is refused with a `::quote/ParseError`,
      whose `:expected` and `:location` tell what the parser wanted and
      where, with `:found` when a token stood there, and `!|` reads it as
      every other error.
      ::link~(::quote/parse | doc) ::link~(::any/apply | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:string/parse/answer
  |~~ The quote of the steps the text spells. ~~|
  ::quote

:string/parse/readsTheSteps
  |~~ The source reads as its steps. ~~|
  ::law~("1 | add 1" | parse | count | eq 2)

:string/parse/readsTheQuote
  |~~ The source reads as the quote it spells. ~~|
  ::law~("add 1 2" | parse | eq ~(add 1 2))

:string/parse/readsTheEmptyQuote
  |~~ The empty text spells the empty quote. ~~|
  ::law~("" | parse | eq ~())

:string/parse/readsBackByParse
  |~~ `parse` on the quote answers the text again. ~~|
  ::law~("add 1" | parse | parse | eq "add 1")

:string/parse/runsByApply
  |~~ `apply` runs the quote on a subject. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | apply ("add 1" | parse) | eq 6)

:string/parse/runsASourceWithItsOwnValueByApply
  |~~ A source that starts from a value of its own runs by `apply /`. ~~|
  ::law~("1 | add 1" | parse | apply / | eq 2)

:string/parse/refusesASourceTheParserRefuses
  |~~ A source the parser cannot read is refused. ~~|
  ::law~("add (" | parse !| type | eq ::quote/ParseError)

:string/parse/namesTheTokenFound
  |~~ The refusal names the token that stood where the parser stopped. ~~|
  ::law~("add )" | parse !| /found | eq ")")
